Make length() count the elements of an array passed to a function.
A function argument is a copy of the caller's variable that refers back
to it as its parent, and the array, with its VF_ARRAY flag, stays with
the parent, so length() saw no array and returned the length of the
empty string: "function f(a) { return length(a) }" returned 0 for any
array. Follow the argument to its parent, as iamarray() does, except
when the argument is the variable of a "for (arg in A)" loop, whose
walker reuses the parent pointer.

---
 editors/awk.c |   16 ++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

--- a/editors/awk.c
+++ b/editors/awk.c
@@ -3758,10 +3758,18 @@
 					L.s = getvar_s(intvar[F0]);
 					debug_printf_eval("length: L.s='%s'\n", L.s);
 				}
-				else if (L.v->type & VF_ARRAY) {
-					R_d = L.v->x.array->nel;
-					debug_printf_eval("length: array_len:%d\n", L.v->x.array->nel);
-					break;
+				else {
+					var *a = L.v;
+					/* An array passed to a function is the parent
+					 * of the argument (but a "for (arg in A)" walker
+					 * reuses x.parent) */
+					while ((a->type & (VF_CHILD | VF_WALK)) == VF_CHILD)
+						a = a->x.parent;
+					if (a->type & VF_ARRAY) {
+						R_d = a->x.array->nel;
+						debug_printf_eval("length: array_len:%d\n", a->x.array->nel);
+						break;
+					}
 				}
 				R_d = strlen(L.s);
 				break;
